## 3. Read vs write vs search: which one to use

Definitions:
- Read = fetch by position or known handle (index, top, front, pointer).
- Search = find by value or key (or by range / prefix).
- Write = insert, delete, update.

### By workload

| Your workload | Best choice | Why | Avoid |
|---|---|---|---|
| Read by index, rarely modified | Array / dynamic array | O(1) index, best cache locality | linked list (O(n) index) |
| Append heavy, read by index | Dynamic array | O(1) amortized append, O(1) read | linked list (pointer overhead) |
| Search by exact key, any write rate | Hash map / hash set | O(1) avg search, insert, delete | tree, unless you need order |
| Search by key and need sorted order, floor / ceiling, or range | Balanced BST (TreeMap) or B+ tree | O(log n) for everything plus ordered iteration | hash map (no order) |
| Search on read-only (static) data | Sorted array + binary search | O(log n), compact, cache friendly, no pointers | rebuilding a tree |
| Heavy insert / delete at the ends | Deque / linked list | O(1) at both ends | array (O(n) front shifts) |
| Insert / delete in the middle with a node handle already held | Doubly linked list | O(1) splice | array (O(n) shift) |
| Always need the min or max | Heap / priority queue | O(1) peek, O(log n) push / pop | sorted array (O(n) insert) |
| Prefix queries / autocomplete | Trie | O(L), independent of n | scanning a hash map of strings |
| Range sums or min / max with updates | Segment tree / Fenwick tree | O(log n) both | prefix sum array (O(n) update) |
| Range sums, no updates | Prefix sum array | O(1) query, O(n) build | segment tree (overkill) |
| "Is it probably in the set?" with tight memory | Bloom filter | tiny memory, O(k) | big hash set |
| Connectivity / grouping queries | Union-Find | near O(1) | BFS on every query |
| Cache with eviction | Hash map + doubly linked list (LRU) | O(1) get and put | plain map (no eviction) |
| Relationships, sparse | Adjacency list | O(V + E) space | matrix (O(V^2)) |
| Relationships, dense or O(1) edge check needed | Adjacency matrix | O(1) edge lookup | list (O(deg) lookup) |
| Very large data on disk, read-heavy | B+ tree | few disk reads, range scans | hash index if you need ranges |
| Very large data on disk, write-heavy | LSM tree (RocksDB, Cassandra) | sequential writes, batched compaction | B+ tree (random writes) |

### Quick comparison: read vs search vs write

| Data structure | Read (by index / top) | Search (by value / key) | Write (insert / delete) | Best at |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Array / dynamic array | O(1) fast | O(n), or O(log n) if sorted | O(n), except append / pop at end | Read |
| Linked list | O(n) | O(n) | O(1) at ends or with node handle | Write at known positions |
| Stack / queue / deque | O(1) at ends | O(n) | O(1) at ends | Ordered processing (LIFO / FIFO) |
| Hash map | O(1) by key | O(1) | O(1) | Search + write |
| Balanced BST | O(log n) | O(log n) | O(log n) | Ordered search + write |
| Heap | O(1) top | O(n) | O(log n) | Priority access |
| Trie | O(L) | O(L) prefix | O(L) | Prefix search |
| B+ tree | O(log_B n) | O(log_B n) + fast range scan | O(log_B n) | Disk-based read + range |

### Decision shortcut

1. Need order or range / floor / ceiling? Balanced BST (or sorted array if read-only).
2. Need only lookup by key? Hash map.
3. Need only the min or max repeatedly? Heap.
4. Need FIFO or LIFO? Queue / stack / deque.
5. Need index access? Array / dynamic array.
6. Need prefix matching? Trie.
7. Need constant-time insert / delete in the middle with a handle? Doubly linked list (usually with a hash map to hold the handles).
8. Need grouping / connectivity? Union-Find.
9. Need range aggregates with updates? Segment tree / Fenwick tree.

### Practical notes

- Hash map worst case O(n) is rare with a good hash function; in interviews state "O(1) average".
- Dynamic array append is O(1) amortized; a single append that triggers a resize is O(n).
- Linked lists often lose to arrays in practice because of cache misses, even where the Big-O looks equal or better.
- Heap `build` from n items is O(n), not O(n log n); n separate pushes is O(n log n).
- Sorting a list once (O(n log n)) then binary searching beats repeated linear scans when searches outnumber about log n.
- Unbalanced BST degrades to a linked list on sorted input; use a balanced one (TreeMap) in production.
